\title{\code{\verb~|ir2lean~} drops fast-math flags without a diagnostic}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{extraction}
\tag{semantics}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-23}
\meta{pr}{6}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{\code{\verb~|ir2lean~} drops fast-math flags without a diagnostic. 45 of the surveyed entrypoints contain \code{\verb~|nnan~} or \code{\verb~|ninf~} on some instruction, among them \code{\verb~|cospif~}, which exports today. The flags are not in the source; the optimizer adds them where it has proved that no NaN or infinity can occur, so they have the same standing as \code{\verb~|nsw~} and \code{\verb~|nuw~}, which \code{\verb~|Semantics.lean~} documents as not represented. No \code{\verb~|nsz~}, \code{\verb~|arcp~}, \code{\verb~|contract~}, \code{\verb~|reassoc~} or \code{\verb~|afn~} occurs anywhere.}
\p{Found by [the survey of extraction](note-extraction-coverage) of \pr{6}; see [the observations about the tools](note-extraction-tools).}
\p{Either reject an instruction that carries \code{\verb~|nnan~} or \code{\verb~|ninf~}, or represent the flags in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/IR/Syntax.lean~} and say in the semantics what an execution that violates one means, as the branch of \pr{13} does for \code{\verb~|nsw~} and \code{\verb~|nuw~}.}
